# §2. Falsification accounting on AO48-DER-007 §5 — GP-AUD-032 was right to fire

**What happened.** DER-007 §5 pre-committed: "any escape with s below its own
threshold kills the sketch." But I never supplied a computable threshold — ρ was
left as an untracked allowance. GP-AUD-032 executed the only machine-defined
projection, ρ=0, and found **three violating escapes.** Its adjudication is
exactly correct on both halves: the ρ=0 threshold is **FALSIFIED**, and the full
statement was not falsified — because it was **unfalsifiable as filed.**

**That second half is the real finding against me, and I log it as error #8:**
a pre-commitment against an uncomputed threshold is not a pre-commitment. It has
the costume of Rule 7 without the content. The three violators
(|a| ≈ 2.0–2.3, |z| ≈ 4.8–7.8) sit precisely where my step-(i) prose gestured —
"quadratic-in-Y terms absorbed… pins \(W_0\)" — without ever writing the term
into the threshold. The missing allowance is the \(|z|F\) term, which
**GP-DER-037 §3 derived correctly**
(\(\rho_1=r|c_{22}|/8+(|z|+r|c_{13}|/2)F+r|c_{04}|F^2/2\); pure-cubic
\(\rho_1=|z||a|/8\)), resolving all three violations with margins +0.988,
+1.829, +0.978.

**Disposition of DER-007 §5:**

- **Step (i), invariant strip:** superseded by GP-DER-037's Lemma — the
  completed, tracked version. Architecture survives; **ownership of the working
  lemma is GP's.** My step-(i) text should no longer be cited.
- **Step (ii), entry; step (iv), capture:** still open as of my read scope, with
  GP-DER-037 §7 additionally exposing a hole my architecture missed entirely —
  strong negative s does **not** control the axial quartic term
  \(r(c_{40}/6)X(X^2-\tfrac14)\), so a typed-margin hypothesis or Gaussian tail
  split is mandatory, not optional. That is a correction to my step (iii) as
  well.
- **The probability step:** superseded in the strong direction by GP-DER-043's
  Theorem 4.1, which proves the O(r³) reduction for *any* polynomial-width
  layer, modulo the named GR premise. Its §9 exponent ledger (+1 +4 −2) also
  refines my "+1 density, +2 determinant" bookkeeping from OQ-005: same total,
  but my "two determinant powers" was the *net* of four raw powers minus the
  normalizer's two. The heuristic was right in sum and imprecise in
  decomposition; the ledger is the citable form.

**The one-repair rule, stated for the record:** my technical deliverables in
AO48-DER-011 (filed alongside this) include a closed-form threshold in the
completed family. If any stored escape violates *that* threshold under
